{{Australian Senators}}

==Electoral prospects: Senate==
A total of 36 senators are not up for re-election: 19 from the Coalition, 14 from Labor, two [[Australian Greens]] and one [[Family First]]. The Coalition needs to win 20 of the 40 contested Senate seats to maintain its Senate majority, while Labor would need to win 25 seats to have a Senate majority in its own right. A Labor majority would require a preferred vote of over 57% in five of the six states; this would be a record for Labor, surpassing the 55.09% it recorded in the [[Australian federal election, 1943|1943 election]]. On recent polling, it appears likely that the balance of power will revert to the minor parties.<ref>[http://www.smh.com.au/news/National/Govt-warns-against-Greens-after-poll/2007/06/26/1182623896172.html]</ref>

The [[Australian Democrats]], who were the minor party with the most Senators from 1978 to 2005, now face electoral oblivion. All four Democrat seats are up for re-election, and two incumbents, [[Andrew Murray (politician)|Andrew Murray]] and [[Natasha Stott Despoja]], have stated that they will not seek a fresh term.<ref>http://www.theage.com.au/news/opinion/so-is-the-party-over/2007/09/22/1189881833182.html</ref> The loss of Stott Despoja, arguably the nation's highest-profile Democrat, will complicate the party's efforts to resuscitate its electoral appeal.

The minor parties with a chance of holding the balance of power are the [[Australian Greens]] and [[Family First]]. With the exception of Greens Senator [[Bob Brown]] who is likely to receive sufficient first preference votes (14.3%) in [[Tasmania]] to secure re-election, each party's success will largely depend on securing favourable preference flows from the major parties and assorted micro-parties. While the Coalition is expected to preference Family First ahead of the Greens, the battle to secure Labor's preferences will be fierce.

Coalition senators [[Paul Calvert]], [[Rod Kemp]], [[Sandy Macdonald]] and [[Kay Patterson]]; and Labor senator [[Robert Ray (Australian politician)|Robert Ray]] will not contest the 2007 election.

===High-profile candidates===
(<!-- Please note that, to avoid the appearance of promoting candiates here, this list is sorted -->Alphabetically by surname.<!-- Please keep it that way -->)

[[Independent (politician)|Independent]] [[Peter Andren]], the Member for [[Division of Calare|Calare]] in NSW, had originally decided to contest the Senate following the substantial changes to the boundaries of his electorate. This would have created significant interest to the senate election in his state. However, following his diagnosis of [[pancreatic cancer]] on [[10 August]], [[2007]], Andren decided to not stand for re-election.<ref>Davis, Mark, [http://www.smh.com.au/news/national/mp-diagnosed-with-cancer/2007/08/10/1186530579998.html "MP diagnosed with cancer"], ''[[Sydney Morning Herald]]'', [[August 10]], [[2007]]. Accessed [[August 10]], [[2007]].</ref>

Former Queensland politician [[Pauline Hanson]] announced recently that she will be standing for the Senate in Queensland. <ref> [http://www.abc.net.au/news/stories/2007/08/15/2005271.htm "Hanson Launches new political party."], ''[[Australian Broadcasting Corporation|ABC]]'', [[August 15]], [[2007]]/ Accessed [[August}}, [[2007]].</ref>

Author and broadcaster Dr [[Karl Kruszelnicki]] is running for the Senate in New South Wales for the [[Climate Change Coalition]], occupying second place on the party's ticket in that state.

[[Andrew Wilkie]] has been pre-selected as second on the ticket for the [[Tasmanian Greens]] behind Senator [[Bob Brown]]. He is a former [[intelligence officer]] who resigned from the [[Office of National Assessments (Australia)|Office of National Assessments]] (ONA) over his concerns about government use of intelligence information in building a case for the [[2003 invasion of Iraq]]. In the [[Australian federal election, 2004|2004 election]] he polled 16% for the [[Australian Greens|Greens]] in Prime Minister [[John Howard]]'s electorate of [[Division of Bennelong|Bennelong]].

[[No Pokies]] MP [[Nick Xenophon]] is running for a South Australian Senate seat. He attracted a fifth of the statewide upper house vote (20.5 percent) at the [[South Australian general election, 2006|2006 state election]]. His platform will consist of anti-gambling, pro-consumer protection, attention to the water crisis, opposition against what he calls a decrease in state rights, and opposition to [[WorkChoices]].<ref>http://www.theaustralian.news.com.au/story/0,25197,22567900-5006787,00.html</ref><ref>http://www.theage.com.au/news/national/fearful-of-xenophon-in-senate/2007/10/11/1191696082652.html</ref>

